  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1031 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nBy Thursday, PWAT values are forecast to be back near 1\", which is\na whole half-inch more than where we are currently sitting. Mid-\nlevel flow will shift southwesterly, advecting deep moisture into\nthe area under this ridge, leading to hot temperatures in the \nupper 80s to low 90s by Friday with dew point temperatures in the \n60s. This will make for uncomfortable conditions and result in \nmoderate Heat Risk, so keep this in mind if you have outdoor \ninterests Friday. Low to medium rain chances will finally enter \nthe forecast Friday afternoon, but if we see rain or not will \ndepend a lot on how much moisture has advected in by that point. \nGoing off of model soundings, profiles show much more moisture\nduring that time, so maybe we're able to squeak some showers out.\nIf we don't and Nashville (BNA) goes the entire day Friday \nwithout measurable rainfall, that will officially break the record\nfor the most consecutive days without rainfall; 36 days is the \ncurrent record.\n\nBy Saturday, rain chances are much higher and there's greater\nconfidence in rain occurring as moisture will still continue \nstreaming in with PWAT values forecast to approach or possibly \nexceed the 2\" mark. That would push above the 95th percentile \nclimatologically, so literally going from one extreme to the \nother. There are widespread 60-70% rain chances during the \nSaturday afternoon/evening - Sunday morning timeframe as multiple\ndisturbances move through the area: a low developing over the \ngulf that will wrap up into the southwesterly flow, a low over the\nsouthwest US sourced from the Pacific that will trek east, and \ntroughing over the north that will usher a shortwave and attendant\ncold front through Sunday into Monday. So a lot to look at and a \nlot of things that could play a role in how much rain we get. On \nthe plus side, with such high PWAT values and various sources of \nforcing, confidence is high in us getting a good, hopefully \nsoaking rainfall this weekend. Ensembles are giving a 50-60% \nchance of greater than 1 inch during this time, and a 20-30% of \ngreater than 2 inches. Given the very dry antecedent conditions, \nsoils will be able to handle a good amount of rainfall, but we'll \nstill monitor for low-lying flooding. Showery conditions look to \nlinger into Monday, but we will dry back out and fall under \nnorthwesterly flow that will drop temperatures back to more \nseasonal values of mid to upper 70s.",
